# models/SOURCE - what the vendored captcha weights are, and their license.

ACTIVE PIN (2026-11-05, owner-directed): the hcaptcha-challenger modelhub
grid library
  project:    hcaptcha-challenger (QIN2DIM), on github.com
  artifacts:  the per-prompt binary tile classifiers from the release
              tagged "model" (the repo's modelhub release; 487 small
              ResNet-style .onnx models, ~300 KB each, one per challenge
              label: cat2309.onnx, dog.onnx, animal2309.onnx, ...)
  release:    https://github.com/QIN2DIM/hcaptcha-challenger/releases/tag/model
  asset URL:  https://github.com/QIN2DIM/hcaptcha-challenger/releases/download/model/<file>.onnx
  sha256:     one pin per file in models/CHECKSUMS (generated after fetch)
  license:    GPL-3.0 (the project's LICENSE)
  use:        downloaded to this machine's disk only, under GPL-3.0's free
              local/private use. NOT redistributed: the .onnx files are
              git-ignored (models/hcaptcha/) and never enter the tree,
              release, or any published artifact. No upstream SOURCE is
              copied into this repo - the runtime code (captcha_model.py,
              models/FEATURIZER.py) is written fresh from the decompiled
              ONNX graphs alone (input/output tensor contracts, op list,
              embedded constants read with the `onnx` protobuf loader),
              with decision rules and preprocessing validated against
              upstream's own ground-truth tile set where such a set exists
              (image_label_binary/off_road_vehicle: 40/40). Anything true
              upstream does that cannot be inferred from the graphs alone
              is disclosed as a gap in FEATURIZER's docstring, not papered
              over.
  graph contract (every sampled model):
      input  "input.1": float32[1, 3, 64, 64]  NCHW RGB, batch pinned to 1
      output (name varies): float32[1, 2] raw logits; class 0 = tile
      matches the challenge prompt
      preprocessing: NONE inside the graph - decode, squash to 64x64, RGB,
      /255 and nothing further (validated 40/40 against ground truth).
  what these models are: the real per-tile relevance answerer for
  hcaptcha/captcha-style image grids - prompt names the model, model sides
  each tile yes/no. They are NOT a multimodal LLM; hcaptcha-challenger's
  newest release line (v0.18+) routes more exotic challenges (relative
  comparisons like "select all numbers smaller than", GAN-era prompt
  families the vintage models never trained on) through its LLM lane
  instead - which is exactly the ladder's llm rung above this cpu rung.

HISTORICAL (superseded by the owner directive of 2026-11-05, kept for the
record): the ONNX Model Zoo's Apache-2.0 SqueezeNet 1.0 stand-in
(squeezenet1.0-12.onnx, pinned at upstream commit
4c46cd00fbdb7cd30b6c1c17ab54f2e1f4f7b177 in CHECKSUMS's previous revision)
was vendored in 1ea4757..8fe5746 under the then-binding Apache-only vendor
rule. It was never a captcha solver - a generic ImageNet classifier whose
cpu rung could only record an honest skip - so its file name
captcha-classifier.onnx is retired here: the active weights under
models/hcaptcha/ answer for real, and the SqueezeNet stand-in story lives
only in this paragraph and the git history.

2026-11-05 MODEL WIRING (the detector + clip rungs): the same release
pinned above (tag "model", 553 assets) also ships the two further weight
families the ladder runs, plus two weight-adjacent data files. All of it
is downloaded to this machine's disk only, verified against the
models/CHECKSUMS pins, and NEVER committed or redistributed.

  models/yolo/  - 59 YOLOv8 object detectors (~36 MB, release assets,
                  GPL-3.0): the coordinate-challenge answerers. The decompile
                  contract and the consumer recipe live in
                  models/YOLO_FEATURIZER.py (validated end to end:
                  COCO2020_yolov8m answers cat p=0.89 / dog p=0.93 with
                  correct boxes under exactly that recipe; the runtime
                  loader re-proves the decode in the suite's real-weights
                  detector test). Routing uses upstream's own ashes_of_war
                  label table, fetched VERBATIM as
                  models/yolo/objects2023.yaml from upstream's src tree and
                  read directly (no derived table file ships or exists here);
                  the 7-anchor-era yolov5/v6 checkpoints and
                  onclick_yolov8m stay vendored inventory - no route speaks
                  for them (different output layout / undisclosed label
                  semantics), disclosed rather than guessed.
  models/clip/  - 3 CLIP encoder pairs (~2.6 GB, release assets, GPL-3.0):
                  visual_CLIP_<family>.onnx + textual_CLIP_<family>.onnx for
                  rn50.openai, ViT-B-32.openai, ViT-L-14-DataComp.XL-s13B-
                  b90K. The decompile contract and preprocessing live in
                  models/CLIP_FEATURIZER.py; the prompt-side BPE tokenizer
                  lives in models/CLIPTOKENIZER.py and re-derives its ids
                  from the vocabulary file below. The runtime matcher
                  (captcha_model.load_clip) L2-normalizes both sides and
                  picks tiles clearing an absolute floor AND the grid's own
                  mean+0.5*std margin; DEFAULT family ViT-B-32 (validated on
                  the vendored 3x3 challenge screenshot: per-tile argmax
                  8/9 - only the heaviest anti-pattern-overlaid tile
                  misreads - and prompt-threshold picks exact 9/9, RN50 7/9
                  and 8/9 respectively; see the suite's real-weights CLIP
                  test). The ViT-L-14 visual half fails the onnx checker on
                  disk (released asset is corrupt upstream; byte-identical
                  to the release, pinned, fetched); ONNXRuntime refuses to
                  build it, the matcher caches that refusal per family and
                  falls to the next family - disclosed, not papered over.
  models/clip/bpe_simple_vocab_16e6.txt.gz
                - CLIP's own BPE vocabulary, from
                  https://github.com/openai/CLIP (MIT license; the one pin
                  here that is not GPL-sourced).
  models/yolo/objects2023.yaml
                - upstream's ashes_of_war table, fetched verbatim from
                  https://raw.githubusercontent.com/QIN2DIM/hcaptcha-challenger/main/src/objects2023.yaml
                  (rung data only; the routing phrase rules that turn the
                  table into prompt-matches are fresh code, as is the rest
                  of the runtime).

Routing phrasing rules (detector_for_prompt): upstream's head and catch-all
matching semantics re-implemented fresh - head labels route "head of the
<animal>", digits route number-two/number-nine phrasings, the special
labels (not-animal, x-eaten, can-run, the-one-piece, animal-head, hexagon,
spiral-center, star-bricks) route their challenge phrasings, everything
else routes on the table's plain labels plus stem-derived species phrases.
KNOWN GAPS, disclosed: bare animal prompts ("click on the owl") may have no
detector route when only a head model exists (the grid library covers
those); upstream's digit-keypoint pixel-fin tune (a few px right/down nudge
for number clicks) is not replicated; prompts the table cannot express
route nothing and fall to the llm/clip rungs honestly.

CLASS FILTER (review fix, 2026-11-05): a located box is clicked only when
its graph class matches the prompt's routed label. The class ids come from
each ONNX graph's own "names" metadata (parsed once per model at session
build); labels whose head model has no plain entry fall back to the
"<label>-head" neighbor once, and any stand-down - a graph without
parseable names metadata, or a routed label with no class id - keeps the
pre-filter behavior but records a WARNING in the
greysearch.captcha.model log, once per model/label/cause. Unmapped labels
with at least one resolved sibling still filter by the resolved class.

VENDOR SCOPE: install.sh fetches and verifies the grid library by default;
GREYSEARCH_CAPTCHA_VENDOR=all adds the yolo/clip pins (~5 GB). A vendor
run NEVER deletes weight files already on disk.
